We observed that neuronal blw KD resulted in an increase in overall brain H2O2 levels (Fig. 3a) and mitochondrial ROS levels (Fig. 3b), as assessed using the Amplex Red assay and mitoSOX staining, respectively. In contrast, pan-neuronal dPGC-1 overexpression reduced brain H2O2 levels (Fig. 3c) and mitochondrial ROS levels (Fig. 3d).Fig. 3
Genetical manipulation of mitochondrial functionality alters brain ROS level
(a) Relative H2O2 levels in 15d elav;tubulin-gal80ts/blw RNAi and control fly heads (n = 10–12 independent animal brains, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗P < 0.05,Unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD);
(b) Quantification of mitoSOX intensity in 15d elav;tubulin-gal80ts/blw RNAi fly brains normalized to the corresponding control (n = 13–14 independent brains; ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01,∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance; Unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD.)
(c) Relative hydrogen peroxide (H2O2) levels in 15d elav/dPGC1 and elav/+ fly heads (n = 10–12 independent animal brains, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗P < 0.05, Unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD);
(d) Quantification of mitoSOX intensity in 15d elav/dPGC1 fly brains normalized to elav/+ control (n = 13–14 independent brains; ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01,∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance; Unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD.)
(e) Survival rate against 5 mM paraquat in elav;tubulin-gal80ts/blw RNAi and control females and males (Data are pooled from three independent biological replicates. ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, Log-rank (Mantel-Cox) test).
(f) Survival rate against 5 mM paraquat in 15d elav/dPGC1 and elav/+ females and males (Data are pooled from three independent biological replicates. ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, Log-rank (Mantel-Cox) test);
(g) Representative confocal images Immunostained for BRPnc82 in 15d elav/+, elav/UAS-Cat, and elav/UAS-SOD2 fly brains; Quantification of BRPnc82 intensity within the central brain region normalized to control (n = 13–14 independent brains; adjusted ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance; n = 13–14 independent animal brains. One-way ANOVA with Bonferroni multiple comparisons test. Error bars: mean ± SD. Scale bar:50um).
(h) Representative confocal images Immunostained for BRPnc82 in 15d elav;tubulin-gal80ts/+, elav;tubulin-gal80ts/blw RNAi, and elav;tubulin-gal80ts/SOD2, blw RNAi fly brains; Quantification of BRPnc82 intensity within the central brain region normalized to control (n = 13–14 independent brains; adjusted ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance; n = 13–14 independent animal brains. One-way ANOVA with Bonferroni multiple comparisons test. Error bars: mean ± SD. Scale bar:50um).Fig. 3

As expected, immunostaining revealed a significant elevation of BRP in the fly brains of 15d-old 3xBRP flies compared to isogenic wild-type controls (Fig. 5a). Subsequently, we conducted an analysis of mitochondrial oxidative phosphorylation rates in young (5d) 3xBRP and isogenic wild-type fly brains using the single-brain seahorse assay. Remarkably, our quantitative OCR measurements demonstrated a drastic reduction in mitochondrial basal respiration in 3xBRP fly brains compared to age-matched wildtype siblings (Fig. 5b). Concomitant with the decline of mitochondrial respiration rates, ATP level in the brains of 3xBRP flies also exhibited a substantial decrease compared to the wild-type controls (Fig. 5c). Additionally, the fluorescent probe-MitoTimer expressed in 3xBRP flies under the pan-neuronal driver elav, revealed a marginally but significantly higher red to green fluorescence ratio in the brains of 3xBRP flies relative to their isogenic wild-type counterparts (Fig. 5d), pointing toward reduced mitochondrial turnover as an explanation of decreased mitochondrial respiration in the brains of 3xBRP flies. While decreased mitochondrial functionality was evident in 3xBRP fly brains, a similar reduction in mitochondrial mass as measured via mtDNA estimates, was not observed (Fig. 5e). Therefore, we speculated that the decline in mitochondrial respiration in the brains of 3xBRP flies might not be attributed to a reduced mitochondrial quantity but rather be explained by abundance changes of mitochondrial proteins.Fig. 5
Genetically-triggered “PreScale” re-programs brain mitochondria
(a) Quantification of confocal immunostaining for BRPnc82 intensity within the central brain region normalized to control in 15d 3xBRP and isogenized w1118 fly brains (n = 13–14 independent brains; ∗∗∗p < 0.001; unpaired t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD)
(b) Basal OCR in fly brains of 5d 3xBRP and wildtype flies. Data are pooled from two independent experiments (n = 22–23 fly brains, ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD)
(c) Relative ATP content in the whole brain of 5d 3xBRP flies normalized to isogenized w1118. (n = 16–18 fly heads, ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD)
(d) Quantification of MitoTimer Red/Green ratio in 5d 3xBRP and wt fly brains, (n = 13–14 independent brains; ∗∗p < 0.01; unpaired t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD);
(e) qPCR of mitochondrial DNA copy number normalized to nuclear DNA in 5d 3xBRP fly brains normalized to wt brains. (n = 30 fly brains per genotype, ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD);
(f–g) Representative confocal images of adult brain immunostained for ATP5A from 5d (f) wt and (g) 3xBRP flies; scale bar:50um;
(h) Quantification of ATP5A intensity within the central brain region normalized to control in 3xBRP and wt fly brains (n = 13–14 independent brains; ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD);
(i–j) Representative confocal images of adult brain immunostained for COXIV from 5d (i) wt and (j) 3xBRP flies, scale bar:50um;
(k) Quantification of COXIV intensity within the central brain region normalized to control in 3xBRP and wt fly brains (n = 13–14 independent brains; ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD);
(l) Quantification of Relative H2O2 levels (the Amplex red absorbance rate normalized to protein level) in 5d wt and 3xBRP fly heads; (n = 19–20 independent animal brains, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗P < 0.05, Unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD);
(m) Quantification of mitoSOX intensity in 5d wt and 3xBRP fly brains (n = 13–14 independent brains; ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01,∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance; Unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD.)
(n–o) Survival rate against 5 mM paraquat in 5d wt and 3xBRP flies; (n) females and (o) males (Data are pooled from three independent biological replicates, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, Log-rank (Mantel-Cox) test).Fig. 5

At the presynaptic terminal, mitochondrial functions are closely tied to Ca2+ buffering [67]. Ca2+ level changes within the mitochondrial matrix are crucial for regulating mitochondrial metabolism and ATP production [68]. Flux of Ca2+ into the mitochondrial matrix is mediated by an electrogenic channel known as the mitochondrial Ca2+ uniporter (MCU). Hence, we initially analyzed the effect of inhibiting mitochondrial Ca2+ uptake on mitochondrial functionality. Subsequently, we suppressed MCU pan-neuronally using the UAS/Gal4 system to express an MCU-directed RNA interference construct and then evaluated the basal respiration rate as well as ATP level in 5d fly brains. Pan-neuronal attenuation of MCU markedly decreased mitochondrial respiration and ATP levels relative to the corresponding controls (Fig. 6a and b). Simultaneously, Amplex Red assay and mitoSOX staining in fly brains were conducted, showing a significant increase in mitochondrial ROS level after neuronal MCU KD (Fig. 6c and d). Thus, pan-neuronal attenuation of the MCU reproduced the mitochondrial phenotype observed in the 3xBRP brains. To further investigate whether neuronally inhibiting MCU might trigger PreScale, we conducted BRP immunostaining. Indeed, neuronally blocking MCU significantly upregulated BRP intensity when compared to their isogenic elav/+ siblings (Fig. 6e). Thus, impaired mitochondrial calcium influx can seemingly trigger the generation of mitochondrial ROS, which subsequently promote the onset of PreScale.Fig. 6
Mitochondrial Ca2+ influx may mediate PreScale-induced mitochondrial reprogramming
(a) Basal OCR was measured in 5d single brain of elav/MCU RNAi and elav/+ animals. Data are pooled from three independent experiments (n = 27–29 fly brains, ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD);
(b) Relative ATP level in the whole brain normalized to corresponding controls in 5d elav/MCU RNAi and elav/+ fly heads; (n = 18–20 fly brains, ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD);
(c) Quantification of Relative H2O2 levels (the Amplex red absorbance rate normalized to protein level) in 5d elav/+ and elav/MCU RNAi fly heads (n = 19–20 independent animal brains, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗P < 0.05, Unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD);
(d) Quantification of mitoSOX intensity in 5d elav/+ and elav/MCU RNAi fly brains (n = 13–14 independent brains; ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01,∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance; Unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD.)
(e) Representative confocal images of adult brain immunostained for BRPnc82 in 5d elav/+ and elav/MCU RNAi flies; Quantification of BRPnc82 intensity within the central brain region normalized to control in 5d elav/+ and elav/MCU RNAi fly brains (n = 13–14 independent brains; ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD); Scale bar:50um;
(f–g) Quantification of relative BRPnc82 intensity within the central brain region normalized to corresponding controls in (f) 5d elav/+ and elav/Itpr RNAi fly brains; (g) in 5d elav/+ and elav/RyR RNAi fly brains; (n = 13–14 independent brains; ∗∗∗p < 0.001, ∗∗p < 0.01, ∗p < 0.05, ns, no significance, unpaired, two-tailed t-test. Error bars: mean ± SD).Fig. 6
